---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { label: "Environments", href: "#environments" },
  { label: "This machine", href: "#local" },
  { label: "WSL", href: "#wsl" },
  { label: "SSH", href: "#ssh" },
  { label: "Usage clock", href: "#usage" },
  { label: "Pop-out chat", href: "#float" },
  { label: "Music", href: "#music" },
  { label: "Open other apps", href: "#apps" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Desktop and environments"
  description="The desktop app is the host: local, WSL, and SSH backends, a usage clock, a pop-out chat window, and listening libraries on this machine."
  currentPath="/docs/desktop"
  eyebrow="Product"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="environments">
    <h2>What an environment is</h2>
    <p>
      An environment is a place Modesto can run agents. The desktop app always
      has a local environment on this computer. You can add a WSL distro or an
      SSH host so projects, provider CLIs, and terminals live there instead of
      on the Windows or macOS side.
    </p>
    <p>
      The right panel lists environments the way Cursor lists them: the active
      backend, its projects, and the surfaces (browser, terminal, files) attached
      to the current thread. Threads bind to an environment id, so a WSL thread
      does not silently edit a Windows path.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Agents run on the environment, not on the phone.</strong>
      <p>
        Pairing a mobile client (see <a href="/docs/remote">Remote access</a>)
        is a window onto a host environment. SSH and WSL are additional hosts
        the desktop can start. Do not confuse a pairing link with an SSH backend.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="local">
    <h2>This machine</h2>
    <p>
      Default. Provider CLIs must be on this process’s <code>PATH</code> (or
      given an explicit binary path). Git, worktrees, and secrets stay under
      <code>~/.modesto</code> unless you set <code>MODESTO_HOME</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="wsl">
    <h2>Windows Subsystem for Linux</h2>
    <p>
      On Windows, add a WSL environment from settings or the command palette
      (“Open WSL folder”). Install Node-unrelated provider CLIs <em>inside</em>
      that distro. The first launch after an app update can take longer while
      Modesto starts the matching backend in WSL.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pick folders from the WSL filesystem. Do not choose a Windows path and
      expect it to be rewritten into Linux for you — the picker is environment
      specific so you do not land the wrong tree.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ssh">
    <h2>SSH hosts</h2>
    <p>
      Add an SSH environment when the agents should run on another machine you
      already reach with SSH. Modesto uses that host’s filesystem and CLIs.
      Auth is your existing SSH config (keys, <code>ProxyJump</code>, and so
      on) plus any password prompt the desktop shows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep provider login on the remote host. A Claude or Codex session started
      over SSH is that machine’s session, not the laptop’s.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="usage">
    <h2>Usage clock</h2>
    <p>
      The header shows a usage clock for the selected model: remaining budget
      on the provider’s 5-hour and weekly windows, when those windows reset,
      and a warning state when you are near the cap. Click it for the bars.
      Limits come from the provider account you already use — Modesto does not
      sell quota.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="float">
    <h2>Pop-out chat</h2>
    <p>
      In the desktop app, <strong>Pop out chat</strong> in the thread header
      opens a compact always-on-top window for that conversation. Use it while
      you work in another app. Closing the floater does not delete the thread;
      it is the same durable timeline as the main window.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="music">
    <h2>Music</h2>
    <p>
      Open the Music surface from the right panel (<kbd>M</kbd>). Modesto
      detects listening apps installed on this machine:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Apple Music (native library on macOS after you grant access)</li>
      <li>Spotify, YouTube Music, TIDAL, SoundCloud, Amazon Music</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On the Mac, Apple Music can list playlists and songs from the local
      library. Other services use the in-app browser, or open in the system
      browser when you are not on desktop. Grant library access per service;
      revoke it from the same panel.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="apps">
    <h2>Open other apps</h2>
    <p>
      From Connections you can launch installed Adobe apps on this machine —
      Photoshop, After Effects, Illustrator, Premiere Pro, InDesign, Lightroom,
      Acrobat, and the rest of that suite — when Modesto finds them in the
      usual install locations. The agent does not remote-control those apps;
      it opens them so you can keep design work beside the thread.
    </p>
    <p>
      Web Connections (Figma, Framer, signed-in sites) are documented under{" "}
      <a href="/docs/work">Work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
